Arch-level exception handlers for aarch64
=========================================

Lives in `module oskit.arch` so the assembly in `vectors.s` can call
a fixed symbol (`oskit_arch__handle_sync_lower`,
`oskit_arch__handle_irq`) regardless of which board is underneath.

Dispatch out of these handlers happens through a small, fixed-name
extern ABI that the board/kernel implementations fulfil:

  arch_dispatch_sync(esr: u64, frame: u64)   — called on every EL0
      sync exception. Handler decides if it's an SVC via esr and
      dispatches accordingly. On unhandled exceptions it's expected
      to abort (or at least not return to a bad state).

  arch_dispatch_irq(frame: u64)              — called on every EL0
      IRQ. Handler ack/EOIs at the platform IRQ controller and
      dispatches to the appropriate IRQ-number handler internally.

  arch_schedule_after_exc(ssp: i64) -> i64   — called after the
      handler returns. Lets the scheduler pick a different thread
      (returning its SSP) or keep the current one (returning `ssp`
      unchanged). The asm path tail-jumps to `arch_resume_process`
      with this value as the new SP_EL1.

Using direct externs instead of module-level function-pointer vars
avoids a sysl codegen bug (variable name mangling is not applied on
the load side when the type is a closure). The bug is noted in memory
so a follow-up can revisit once it's fixed.

    module oskit.arch

    extern read_esr_el1() -> u64

    extern arch_dispatch_sync(esr: u64, frame: u64)
    extern arch_dispatch_irq(frame: u64)
    extern arch_schedule_after_exc(ssp: i64) -> i64

Synchronous exception from EL0. Read ESR, hand off to the platform's
sync dispatcher, then consult the scheduler.

    handle_sync_lower(frame: u64) -> i64
        val esr = read_esr_el1()
        arch_dispatch_sync(esr, frame)
        arch_schedule_after_exc(i64(frame))

Async IRQ from EL0. Hand off to the platform's IRQ dispatcher, then
consult the scheduler.

    handle_irq(frame: u64) -> i64
        arch_dispatch_irq(frame)
        arch_schedule_after_exc(i64(frame))
